// 060 — Spread / splat operator — DESIGN NOTE, what not to do
//
// Python: `foo(*args, **kwargs)`. JavaScript: `foo(...xs)`. Ruby: `*a`.
// These let you unpack a collection into a function's argument slots
// with a single character. Convenient. Also, at the call site, you no
// longer know how many arguments are being passed — "it depends on
// what's in the collection." Behavior becomes runtime-shape-dependent.
//
// AiLang has no spread operator and no variadic functions. If a
// function needs a variable number of inputs, it takes ONE Address
// pointing at a collection (array, XArray, linked list) and the
// function iterates it explicitly. The caller packs, the callee
// unpacks — both steps are code you can read.
//
// Shown below: the AiLang idiom for "pass a variable list of values
// to a function" — pass the collection, iterate in the body.
LibraryImport.Arena

Function.SumBytes {
    Input: buf: Address
    Input: n: Integer
    Output: Integer
    Body: {
        total = 0
        i = 0
        WhileLoop LessThan(i, n) {
            total = Add(total, GetByte(buf, i))
            i = Add(i, 1)
        }
        ReturnValue(total)
    }
}

SubRoutine.Main {
    buf = Allocate(5)
    SetByte(buf, 0, 10)
    SetByte(buf, 1, 20)
    SetByte(buf, 2, 30)
    SetByte(buf, 3, 40)
    SetByte(buf, 4, 50)

    PrintMessage("sum of 5 bytes: ")
    PrintNumber(SumBytes(buf, 5))
    PrintMessage("\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
